\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Closure ist die Eigenschaft, dass beim Erzeugen von Bäumen immer gültige
 Ausdrücke entstehen und eine wichtige Voraussetzung für GP-algorithmen
 die sowohl Zahlen- als auch Wahrheitswerte in Formeln kombinieren können.
 Dafür gibt es zwei Strategien, schwache und starke Typisierung.
 Bei der schwachen Typisierung werden Zahlenwerte beispielsweise über ihr
 Vorzeichen interpretiert und Wahrheitswerte als Binärzahlen.
 Dieser Ansatz wird zwar in vielen Programmiersprachen genutzt, beim suchen
 klassischer mathematischer Formeln ist ein häufiges und automatisiertes
 Wechseln aber unüblich und verwirrend, weshalb Strongly Typed Genetic Programmi
ng (STGP) immer häufiger genutzt wird.
 Eine Umwandlung zwischen Zahlen und Wahrheitswerten erfolgt explizit, either
 by if-statements or comparison operators.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Um Konflikte zu vermeiden müssen Äste, Operatoren und Terminale ihren Datentype
n zugeordnet werden, außerdem muss bei der Crossover-evolution auf Konflikte
 beim Wählen der Äste geachtet werden.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evaluation safety ist ein eine Erweiterung des Problems der Auswertbarkeit
 von Formeln da selbst typkorrekte Ausdrücke mathematisch ungültig sein
 können.
 Bei einer division durch Null wird, wegen dem häufigen Vorkommen, oft ein
 Dummywert (beispielsweise 1) zurückgegeben, oft wird der Baum aber auch
 einfach verworfen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GP offers many expansion possibilities.
 For example, fuzzy GP policies can be searched for, where even complex
 policies with many rules can be searched for without the search space becoming
 so uncontrollably large that solutions can no longer be found.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hein_fuzzy_gp"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Post-hoc explanation 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Online-offline_regression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GP can also be used to search for interpretable post-hoc formulas that represent
 the behaviour of a black box.
 Data with transition samples are created online by the black box policy
 on the real benchmark on which a symbolic regression with GP is performed.
 Due to the far lower complexity, these are subject to losses but can be
 interpreted a lot better.
 How severe these losses are depend on many factors.
 As Hein has found out in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "p. 125"
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, policies that have been trained on offline data generated by a neuropolicy
 are almost always significantly worse than both the neuropolicy and gp
 solutions that were trained on real system dynamics.
 Surrogate models are nevertheless useful as they can be evaluated cost-effectiv
ely and in parallel, but especially with autopilots or industrial controllers,
 the risk of gross misexploration, which could even destroy the benchmark
 itself, is reduced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Trusting Domain Experts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the pinnacle of explainable intelligent approaches, human-written approaches
 represent.
 In many areas, it is therefore up to domain experts to find solution strategies.
 The main reasons to trust an expert over a machine learning approach are
 important to consider for achieving better explainability, because humans
 also have a long learning process behind them before they can be considered
 experts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The human brain, with almost 90 billion neurons, is not only far more complex
 than artificial neural networks, the architecture has been perfected in
 many areas in an evolutionary process lasting millions of years.
 This enables humans to learn a wide range of skills in a very diverse learning
 process, while being severely limited in time and exploration compared
 to machines.
 Image recognition, for example, is perfected in early childhood to such
 an extent that it hardly needs any explanation.
 The learning process is intrinsically continuously monitored by primal
 fears and drives, dangers are analysed and the learned models are thus
 endowed with a high degree of security.
 Humans are also as lazy as they can be and, instead of making an effort
 to find new concepts for a problem, they try to solve a problem with what
 they have already learned.
 Language, as well as the ability to speak, is learned as a child and at
 the same time describes everything that a person can understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
(Ludwig Wittgenstein, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Die Grenzen meiner Sprache bedeuten die Grenzen meiner Welt.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wittgenstein1953philosophical"
literal "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpersonal communication and the ability to implement successful models
 in all people (such as the Pythagorean theorem) also avoids over- and underfitt
ing as much as possible.
 As a communication protocol, a language works because the experiences between
 people are similar enough to be learned and comparable.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
 This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An artificial neural network has to do without all these properties as far
 as possible.
 Through an adapted network architecture and the enormous computing power,
 a specialist idiot can nevertheless be trained to master the given task.
 In contrast to humans, a neural network is dependent on random exploration,
 which can find bias-free policies, but represents a tradeoff to the familiar
 learning process of humans.
 Solutions that are searched for with the aim of minimal representation
 in a language space as defined above can therefore be interpreted by humans,
 but since the search process is also based on chance, the formulas found
 are also free of human bias - but they still often appear to the observer
 more as a random combination of various operators than as a logically abstracte
d solution to a problem.
 
