\begin_layout Chapter
Familiarity based Genetic Programming
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this work, a GP suite in the language python was developed which provides
 the main requisites type consistancy, evaluation safety and closure while
 applying evolution procedures on a origin tree taking into account the
 well-known principles of GP which were already described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "gp_Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset

.
 These should not differ in their behaviour from that of a classic GP if
 an original is left out.
 It was developed by Simon Fehrer with the Help of Daniel Hein and Steffen
 Udluft.
 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://github.com/ducktalez/plagih
\end_layout

\end_inset

 xx (currently not available, private repository)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
sympy vereinfachung, Allgemeine mathematische Vereinfachung with symbolic
 mathematics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evaluation process was inspired by KarooGP, a GP-suite written by Kai
 Staats.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "karooGPpaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 To evaluate a Gp-tree, first the mathematical expression is extracted and
 mathematically simplified with the help of Sympy, a python library for
 symbolic mathematics [xx].
 This eliminates unnecessary mathematical calculations before the evaluation.
 The reduced expression is then used to generate the AST with the python
 compiler, which is recursively converted into a Tensorflow graph (TF).
 This enables parallel evaluation on the graphics card for a 2-15x speedup
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "karooGPpaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, which by using recursive tree structures could even surpass this extraordinary
 performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[xx es gibt keine Statistik über die Laufzeit, allerdings hat sich nach
 dem Verwenden rekursiver Strukturen die Performance mindestens um 50% verbesser
t][xxx Reicht als GP-Quelle Koza und ein Buch?]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
xxx sympy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sympy can also be used to store trees in a simplified way to reduce their
 complexity and accommodate a lengthy optimisation process based on random
 mutation.
 Sympy not only simplifies mathematical expressions, it also always presents
 them in the same ordered version.
 This feature accommodates a problem of TED, which can not identify mathematical
 relations such as the commutative law.
 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $(a+b)=(b+a)$
\end_inset

 is mathematically equivalent, but the TED between the two representations
 is 2.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The AST is built again from the reduced sympy expression, but instead of
 generating a TF graph, a new GP tree is built.
 This algorithm is very error-prone.
 On the one hand, the simplest version of a formula, from a mathematical
 point of view, does not have to be the shortest tree representation, and
 on the other hand, a simplified formula can contain elements that are not
 intended when generating candidates.
 [xx Gödel'scher Unvollständigkeitssatz?] Newly found Pareto entries are
 always tried to be stored simplified.
 The nodes are randomly selected from different distributions.
 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To obtain the most useful set of constants possible, these are chosen at
 random from 100 variables of the data set, Gaussian-distributed integers
 and Gaussian-distributed floating point values.
 
\begin_inset Formula $X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

 With the help of the evolutionary mutation of the Gaussian filters, the
 constants of the best trees are additionally modified to fine-tune.
 For the runs of this work, a Gaussian filter with a standard deviation
 of 0.1 was applied.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $X\sim\mathcal{N}(x,0.1)$
\end_inset

xx)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the introduction of the if operator, which is both the only operator
 with arity-3 and two different input types (boolean condition and numeric
 result), strong typing for all inputs and outputs as well as an extension
 of binary tree structures to any arity became necessary.
 However, due to the high variance in the branching factor, the tree depth
 also loses its function as a limiting factor in creating random branches,
 which is why all procedures based on the tree depth were replaced with
 methods based on the number of nodes.
 [xx pseudocode?] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED is calculated both a robustly and efficiently with APTED.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedAPTED1,tedAPTED2"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 TED mapping between trees can be used in addition to weighting the changes,
 but was not used for any runs.
 This property can prevent closure in certain constellations, which is why
 this possibility is checked and a mutual exclusive crossover was implemented,
 which always finds two matching nodes in trees.
 
\end_layout

